  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Non-metal oxides are acidic in nature as they produce acidic solution by reacting with water, so here&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Cl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;are acidic oxides which produce&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>HOCl</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi>PO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;respectively, with water.</p><p>Alkali metal and alkaline earth metal oxides are basic in nature, as&nbsp;they produce metal hydroxide&nbsp;solution with water and metal hydroxides are basic in nature.</p><p>Here,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Na</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>CaO</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>Li</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>MgO</mi></math>&nbsp;are basic hydroxides which form&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>NaOH</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Ca</mi><msub><mfenced><mi>OH</mi></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>LiOH</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Mg</mi><msub><mfenced><mi>OH</mi></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;respectively, with water.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Al</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math> is amphoteric oxide as it can react either with acid or base to form salt and water.</p>"
